{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":3078,"output":9563,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":7944,"totalTokens":12641,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me go through each item.\n\nQ041: \"Accept these selections\" → \"确认法术选择\". \"Accept these selections\" — no \"法术\" (spell) in source; missing \"these\"? Actually \"确认法术选择\" adds \"法术\" (spell). ADDITION? The source is generic \"Accept these selections\". Target adds \"法术\". Hmm, but could be context-specific. Per rules, addition of info compatible with visible evidence and can't be judged wrong by visible evidence isn't a defect. Is \"法术\" compatible? We can't tell what selections are. Hmm. \"Accept\" → \"确认\" fine. \"these\" omitted. I'd say ADDITION of \"法术\" is a possible FINDING? The rule 3: 新增但与可见证据相容、且无法由可见证据判错的具体信息，不构成缺陷. Since we can't verify selections aren't spells, adding \"法术\" can't be judged wrong from visible evidence → not a defect. Also omission of \"these\" is minor. I'd give empty or maybe ADDITION UNCERTAIN? \"these selections\" → \"法术选择\" - the demonstrative dropped. That's a minor omission; likely not substantive. Empty candidates.\n\nQ042: \"Got to level 10 of the infinite dungeon.\" → \"到达无尽地下城第10层。\" Correct.\n\nQ043: \"THAT'S NOT A VERY HERMIT-LIKE ATTITUDE.\" → \"这可不像我这个隐士该有的态度。\" Adds \"我\" (my). Source: \"that's not a very hermit-like attitude\" — could be about anyone. Target says \"我这个隐士\". Hmm, in Tales of Maj'Eyal this is a hermit NPC speaking. But by visible evidence, \"我\" is added. Is it judgeable? The source doesn't specify whose attitude. Adding \"我\" specifies subject. Could be judgeable as SUBJECT addition... but per rule 3, can't be judged wrong from visible evidence? Actually the subject change: source \"THAT'S NOT A VERY HERMIT-LIKE ATTITUDE\" — the speaker calling themselves the hermit is plausible. But it could also be addressed to player. It's ambiguous. I'd flag as UNCERTAIN SUBJECT? Hmm. Actually the target \"我这个隐士\" explicitly claims speaker is a hermit. The source just says hermit-like attitude, doesn't say speaker is hermit. Visible evidence doesn't contradict but also doesn't support. Rule 3 says compatible additions that can't be judged wrong aren't defects. So empty. Hmm, but strictly it could be judged: source doesn't say \"I\". Yet rule 3 protects compatible additions. I'll leave empty or UNCERTAIN. I'll give empty candidates.\n\nQ044: \"Have 100 walls on the sludgenest turn into hostile creatures.\" → \"让淤泥巢穴中的 200 面墙变成敌对生物。\" 100 → 200. Clear VALUE FINDING.\n\nQ045: \"But what is all th...\" → \"但是这一切到底是……\" Fine.\n\nQ046: \"Killed a Bringer of Doom.\" → \"杀死一名厄运行者。\" Name translation, fine.\n\nQ047: Long lore text. Check: \"ruined from your fight with the creature carrying it\" → \"看来在你刚才和那个人类搏斗的时候被打碎了\". Source: \"the creature carrying it\" — target says \"那个人类\" (that human). Source doesn't say human. Is it judgeable? The document mentions 'sun paladin' species which... external knowledge says sun paladins are human, but we can't use external knowledge. By visible text, \"creature\" ≠ \"human\". Actually the text says pheromone ineffective on species 'sun paladin' — sun paladin could be the creature. But target \"人类\" is a specific addition not supported. Hmm, can it be judged wrong? The source says \"creature\"; the letter discusses 'sun paladin' species. A sun paladin might be human but not stated. Rule 3 protects compatible additions. \"人类\" is compatible-ish. Hmm, but \"creature carrying it\" — carrying the paper? It was carrying the scraps. Target says \"打碎了\" (smashed) vs \"ruined\". Minor. Also \"emigration program should be cleaning of local 'sun paladin' nest\" — target: \"移民计划的下一步是清理本地的\"太阳骑士\"巢穴并建立…\" matches \"next step ... should be cleaning ... and establishment of...\". OK.\n\nMain potential finding: \"creature carrying it\" → \"那个人类\". Also \"an era... \" no. Also \"much Eyalite wildlife\" → \"大部分埃亚尔野生生命体\" fine.\n\nIs \"人类\" a defect? Source says \"creature\". The creature carrying the paper is likely the sun paladin (the writer studied them). Actually the writer is an alien studying Eyal; the creature carrying the notes... In ToME, this is the sunwall-related lore from an alien. The creature was probably a sun paladin human. But by strict rules, \"human\" is added info. Can it be judged wrong from visible evidence? The letter says sun paladin is a species; whether human is unknown. Compatible → not defect per rule 3. Hmm, I'd flag UNCERTAIN? I think UNCERTAIN SUBJECT/REFERENT is defensible. Actually let me reconsider rule 3: \"新增但与可见证据相容、且无法由可见证据判错的具体信息，不构成缺陷。\" Since we can't judge it wrong, not a defect. So empty. But \"那个人类\" vs \"the creature\" — subject referent changed from generic creature to human. I'll mark UNCERTAIN REFERENT maybe. Hmm. Actually also \"被打碎了\" — the paper was ruined, not smashed... \"被打碎了\" for paper, minor. Also \"和那个人类搏斗\" — fight with the creature carrying it. OK.\n\nI'll report UNCERTAIN for 人类? Given rule 3, I lean empty. But let me think: rule 3 is about 新增具体信息 compatible with evidence. \"人类\" is compatible (sun paladin could be human). So no defect. Empty candidates? Also \"看来\" added, fine. I'll give empty.\n\nQ048: fine.\n\nQ049: \"When you do a mouse gesture (right click + drag) a color coded trail is displayed.\" → \"当你用右键拖动画出鼠标手势时，始终会显示彩色的轨迹提示。\" ADDITION: \"始终\" (always). Source: \"When you do... is displayed\" — conditional; target says \"always displayed\". \"始终\" changes condition/timing. FINDING? Source says it's displayed when you do a gesture; target says \"always will display\" — that's an addition of 始终. Actually \"始终会显示\" could be interpreted as \"will always be displayed (when you do the gesture)\". Hmm. It's within the conditional clause \"当你...时\". So \"when you gesture, always displays\" — 始终 emphasizes within that condition. That's arguably compatible. But it could be read as changing meaning. I'd say it's a subtle ADDITION; the \"始终\" is redundant but within scope. Hmm. I'd report UNCERTAIN ADDITION? Or FINDING? The Chinese \"当你...时，始终会显示\" — reads \"whenever you do gesture, always displays trail\". Source: same essentially. I think it's fine, not a defect. But wait — source: \"a color coded trail is displayed\" — color-coded = 彩色, but \"color coded\" means colors have meaning; 彩色 loses \"coded\". Minor. I'll leave empty or UNCERTAIN for 始终. I'll include UNCERTAIN ADDITION for \"始终\".\n\nQ050: Long fountain text. Check omissions: \"a dwarf manipulating earthen runes\" — fine. \"The runes and the fireball are not connected to anything, and float in a fixed position in the air.\" → \"那些符文与火球未与任何东西相连，却保持在空中的固定位置。\" fine. Carvings: \"ALL ARE WELCOME WHO LEARN FROM THE MISTAKES OF THE PAST\" → fine. \"LET THE ARCANE ONCE MORE BE A BOON AND NOT A BANE TO MAJ'EYAL\" → fine. \"MAY WE HAVE THE WISDOM TO ONE DAY REUNITE THE WORLD IN SAFETY AND ENLIGHTENMENT\" → fine. \"GATHER HERE IN PEACE TO DISCOVER AND PERFECT THE KNOWLEDGE OF HOW, AND WHEN, TO WEAVE THE WORLD'S FABRIC\" → \"在此和平相聚，探索并完善何时、如何编织世界织物的知识\" fine. Target adds \"位于德斯镇\" (in Derth) — ADDITION not supported by source. Can it be judged wrong? Source doesn't mention a town. Rule 3: compatible addition? Is \"德斯镇\" compatible with visible evidence? Nothing contradicts. Hmm — but it's a specific location claim. By rule 3, can't be judged wrong from visible evidence → not a defect? This is a location addition. Rule 3 protects it. Hmm, but is it compatible? The description doesn't say where. Adding a specific town name... visible evidence neither supports nor contradicts. Per rule 3, not a defect. Ugh, these rules are strict. Actually wait — the fountain description with Linaniil... In ToME this fountain... Linaniil is in Elvala? Actually the Fountain of Axen Linaniil... Hmm, external knowledge forbidden. I'll not report.\n\nQ051: \"bring me a gem and a ring\" → \"一颗宝石和一枚项链\" — 项链 = necklace, but later \"镶嵌到戒指里\" (ring). Source says ring. Target says 项链 then 戒指. That's an inconsistency: VALUE/REFERENT FINDING. \"项链\" should be \"戒指\". FINDING.\n\nAlso \"There is a small fee dependent on the level of the ring\" → \"根据戒指等级我要收取一小笔费用\" fine. \"you need a quality ring to use a quality gem\" → \"你得寻找高品质的戒指来用于高品质的宝石\" — \"寻找\" added, minor. Main finding: 项链.\n\nQ052: \"But then where are they now if they won?\" → \"兽人是胜利了，但是现在他们又在哪里呢？\" Adds \"兽人\" (orcs). \"they\" — referent unspecified. Adding 兽人 — is it judgeable? Without context, \"they\" referent unknown; 兽人 is a guess. Visible evidence can't confirm. Rule 3 protects if compatible... but it specifies a referent not in evidence. Hmm. Could be judged? The source \"they\" ambiguous; target claims orcs. Since evidence can't confirm orcs, per rule 3, it can't be judged wrong → not defect. But wait, rule 3 requires compatibility; \"they won\" — who? Unknown. Compatible. I'll skip, or UNCERTAIN REFERENT. Given rule 3, skip. Hmm, but actually the instruction says additions that can't be judged wrong aren't defects. Since we can't determine \"they\" isn't orcs, skip. Empty.\n\nQ053: \"Return in a few days, and I'll have everything prepared.\" → \"几天后你回来，我肯定把一切都准备好了。\" \"肯定\" added, minor. \"which the men of Sholtar sealed many years ago\" → \"那是多年前肖尔塔人封印的地方\" fine. \"return with it and your odds of surviving our portal attempt will go up drastically\" → \"请帮我带回来，这能大大提高你通过传送门时存活的几率\" fine. \"If you happen to find\" → \"要是你...找到\" fine. \"别害怕\" for \"Fear not\" fine. OK, empty. Hmm \"它\" refers to key — \"它可以打开泰尔玛废墟\" — actually the key opens the ruins; fine.\n\nQ054: fine.\n\nQ055: \"The Right thing to do\" → \"放走鲜血大师才是正确的选择\" — Adds \"放走鲜血大师\" (letting the Bloodmaster go). Source is just \"The Right thing to do\" (achievement name). Target adds specific content. Can it be judged wrong? Not from visible evidence; but it's a big addition. Rule 3... Hmm. The source has no mention of Bloodmaster. Adding it is a specific claim. Compatible? We can't judge. Per rule 3, not a defect? That seems odd but rules are rules. Hmm, but rule 3 says \"与可见证据相容、且无法由可见证据判错\" — both conditions. The addition is compatible (nothing contradicts). So not a defect. But I feel this is a case where translation added context-specific info. Many evals would flag ADDITION. But the rules explicitly protect it... Actually wait — is it truly \"无法由可见证据判错\"? We have no other context in this request. So yes, can't be judged wrong. Skip. Hmm, but I could report UNCERTAIN ADDITION. Rule 4: UNCERTAIN only when evidence points to problem but genuine ambiguity remains. The addition is clearly unsupported by source... I think reporting UNCERTAIN ADDITION for Q055 and Q052 is defensible. Let me decide: For Q055, the source is an achievement name \"The Right thing to do\"; target elaborates \"放走鲜血大师才是正确的选择\". This is a substantive addition — the translation specifies an action not in source. I'll report UNCERTAIN ADDITION. Actually rule 3 explicitly says such additions 不构成缺陷. \"新增但与可见证据相容、且无法由可见证据判错的具体信息，不构成缺陷。\" This directly covers it. So no candidate. But is it \"与可见证据相容\"? \"The Right thing to do\" — could be about releasing a blood master; nothing in evidence contradicts. So yes. Skip.\n\nSimilarly Q052 兽人 skip. Q058 adds \"即使此处没有抛射物\" (even if there's no projectile here) — that's a specific addition. Source: \"Lua inspect [Projectile]\" — a debug command label. The addition explains behavior. Compatible? Can't be judged wrong from evidence... Actually \"即使此处没有抛射物\" — could be judged? Source gives no info about whether it requires a projectile. Compatible → skip per rule 3. Hmm, but this is a menu item translation adding explanation. Rule 3 protects. Skip.\n\nQ059: \"Removed 89 beneficial effects from enemies via Disintegration.\" → \"必须单次使用裂解清除敌人身上的89个增益效果\". Source: past achievement statement \"Removed 89 ... via Disintegration\". Target: \"必须单次使用\" (must in a single use) — adds condition \"single use\" not in source. Source says removed 89 via Disintegration (could be cumulative?). Actually ToME achievement \"Disintegration\" requires removing effects in... The source visible text doesn't say \"single use\". Target adds \"必须单次\". Is it judgeable wrong? Source doesn't state single use; target adds a requirement. That's ADDITION/CONDITION not supported. Can we judge it wrong? The source is a stat/achievement description \"Removed 89 beneficial effects from enemies via Disintegration.\" — it's descriptive of an action done. Target converts to a requirement \"必须单次使用裂解清除...89个\". Changes modality: statement → requirement. Also \"单次\" added. I'd flag FINDING CONDITION/ADDITION? Evidence: \"Removed 89 beneficial effects from enemies via Disintegration.\" Target adds \"必须单次\". The modality change: source is a record of accomplishment; target is a requirement. Hmm, achievements in ToME are often displayed as \"Removed X ... via Disintegration\" as progress counters. The addition of \"必须单次\" is substantive. Report FINDING: claim_type ADDITION (or CONDITION). Evidence: \"Removed 89 beneficial effects from enemies via Disintegration.\" Target span: \"必须单次使用裂解清除\". Correction: \"通过裂解从敌人身上移除了89个增益效果\". Verdict FINDING — the \"必须单次\" cannot be derived from source. Actually can it be \"compatible and unjudgeable\"? The source describes something already done (past tense \"Removed\"). Target says \"必须...清除\" (must remove) — a requirement. That's a modality mismatch judgeable from evidence: past accomplishment vs requirement. FINDING.\n\nQ060: \"Killed oneself as a halfling.\" → \"选择半身人种族并杀死敌人。\" Source: killed oneself (suicide) as a halfling. Target: killed enemies. DIRECTION/ACTION FINDING. \"Killed oneself\" → \"杀死敌人\" — wrong. FINDING: target_span \"杀死敌人\", correction \"杀死自己/自杀\". claim_type ACTION or DIRECTION. Evidence \"Killed oneself as a halfling.\" FINDING.\n\nQ056: Long text. \"realizing it's actually a human inside that hulking, worn armor\" → \"才发现那副庞大而破旧的战甲里其实装着弗莉吉亚的弟弟。\" Target replaces \"a human\" with \"弗莉吉亚的弟弟\" (Firigia's brother?). Hmm — source says \"a human\". Target names a specific person. Is this judgeable? Source just says a human. Target adds specific identity not in source. Per rule 3, compatible addition... Can't judge wrong from visible evidence. Hmm. But also the gibberish: \"f...t...ma....ll...\" → \"弗……特……玛……尔……\" transliteration, fine. \"It wasn't there just a second ago.\" → \"一瞬之前那里还空无一物。\" fine. \"equally massive battle axe\" → \"同样手持巨大战斧\" — \"equally massive\" modifies armor? \"a massive battle armor with an equally massive battle axe\" — target \"一副同样手持巨大战斧的巨型战甲\" — \"同样\" placement differs slightly but conveys axe equally massive. Fine.\n\nThe \"弗莉吉亚的弟弟\" — ADDITION. In ToME, this is the Temporal Rift event... Actually \"f...t...ma....ll...\" — hmm. Could the armor contain a specific named character? External knowledge forbidden. Per rule 3, \"a human\" → \"弗莉吉亚的弟弟\" specifies identity; compatible? Nothing contradicts. Hmm. But wait — is it compatible? Source says \"a human inside that hulking, worn armor\". \"弗莉吉亚的弟弟\" is a human presumably. Compatible. Per rule 3, not a defect. Ugh. But this feels like a substantive unsourced addition. The rules say only report objective issues supported by visible text. The addition of an identity can't be verified. I could mark UNCERTAIN. Rule 4: UNCERTAIN when evidence points to a problem but genuine ambiguity. Evidence points? Source says \"a human\"; target names someone. It's an addition of identity — I think UNCERTAIN ADDITION/REFERENT is reasonable. Hmm, but rule 3 explicitly shields it. I'll skip per rule 3... Actually let me reconsider rule 3's spirit: it protects translators who add context-appropriate details that can't be falsified. But naming a specific character is risky. Still, per the letter of rules, skip. Hmm.\n\nActually, I'm torn. Let me weigh: these controlled-confirmation evals — rule 3 exists precisely to avoid false positives on context-informed translations (